  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nAs of 215 PM Wednesday afternoon, midlevel troughing was present \nacross the eastern CONUS with ridging across the west. A shortwave \ntrough, responsible for the frontal passage earlier today, is \nadvancing through the mid-MS valley. A modest pressure gradient in \nits wake is contributing to northwesterly winds at 10-20 mph with a \nfew gusts up to 30 mph. These winds will relax to less than 10 mph \nwith sunset as the boundary layer decouples. An area of surface high \npressure will nose into eastern KS late tonight into Thursday \nmorning. With light winds and small dew point depressions, patchy \nfog is possible across much of the area. Overnight lows will range \nfrom the upper 30s to low 40s. This area of surface high pressure \nwill remain overhead into Thursday with light winds and temperatures \nnoticeably cooler in the upper 50s to low 60s. A few midlevel \nclouds are possible as a speed max translates southeast across \nIA/MO. \n\nBeyond Thursday, midlevel ridging will slide across the Plains ahead \nof the next midlevel trough across the west. The area of surface\nhigh pressure will remain overnight Thursday night and Friday\nmorning; light winds and small dew point depressions may yield \npatchy fog once again. The aforementioned midlevel trough axis \nis expected to eject into the central/southern Plains late \nSaturday into Sunday. Latest deterministic and ensemble guidance\nhave trended towards a weaker trough/low with a more-southern \ntrack across OK. This trend will need to be monitored over the \ncoming days as the prospects for widespread rainfall may be \ndecreasing. Transitioning into next week, an active midlevel \npattern may emerge with multiple storm systems potentially \nimpacting the central Plains. The first shortwave trough may \narrive towards the middle of next week with a second towards the\nfollowing weekend.",
